abstract: >-
  SessionHistoryServer records collaborative activity as queryable,
  timed events. Everything a participant does — voice, video, notes,
  edits — lands as events on a shared timeline: instant events,
  duration events with a tentative→updated→closed lifecycle, and
  state events. Media is just events with an attached append-only
  resource: a WebRTC ingest records camera, microphone and screen
  into Matroska files that are servable over HTTP while still
  growing. Pluggable processors (including sandboxed JavaScript and
  OpenAI-compatible LLM processors with schema-validated tool calls)
  subscribe to event streams and derive new events, live or over
  recorded sessions. SessionHistoryServer is the session-history
  component of Stund, an activity-enriched collaborative workspace.
keywords:
  - activity-enriched computing
  - collaboration
  - event sourcing
  - session recording
  - WebRTC
  - GStreamer
  - malleable software
  - AI-as-medium
